With error control on, `CenicIntegrator::DoStep()` linearizes the external systems once, for the full step of size h from x₀ ([cenic_integrator.cc:408-429](https://github.com/RobotLocomotion/drake/blob/c15e0f0372fd730e2f09f1a301abea8fb2c24edb/multibody/cenic/cenic_integrator.cc#L408-L429)), and reuses that linearization for both half-steps ([line 483](https://github.com/RobotLocomotion/drake/blob/c15e0f0372fd730e2f09f1a301abea8fb2c24edb/multibody/cenic/cenic_integrator.cc#L483) and [lines 498-501](https://github.com/RobotLocomotion/drake/blob/c15e0f0372fd730e2f09f1a301abea8fb2c24edb/multibody/cenic/cenic_integrator.cc#L498-L501)). The comment there says the reuse is deliberate, which makes sense for linearizations computed by finite differences. But the same linearization also carries the analytic terms for an actuator's PD controller driven by the `desired_state` input ([icf_external_systems_linearizer.cc:204-236](https://github.com/RobotLocomotion/drake/blob/c15e0f0372fd730e2f09f1a301abea8fb2c24edb/multibody/contact_solvers/icf/icf_external_systems_linearizer.cc#L204-L236)):

```
Kᵤ = Kp⋅h + Kd
bᵤ = −Kp⋅(q₀ − qd) + Kd⋅vd    (with the sign fix in #25290)
```

Those depend on the step size and the starting position, so reusing them changes what the half-steps compute:

- **First half-step** (size h/2, from q₀): Kp⋅h instead of Kp⋅h/2 evaluates the servo's spring at q₀ + h⋅v, where a full step would end, instead of at the half-step's end, q₀ + (h/2)⋅v.
- **Second half-step** (size h/2, from q_{h/2}): bᵤ still uses q₀ instead of q_{h/2}, and Kᵤ still has Kp⋅h, which together again evaluate the spring at q₀ + h⋅v.

So both half-steps evaluate the spring about where the full step does, and the error estimate, which compares the two, misses much of the spring's discretization error. The damping terms (Kd, vd) don't depend on h or q₀, so they're unaffected.

### Measured effect

A lightly damped position servo (I = 0.004 kg⋅m², Kp = 100, Kd = 0.1, so ωn = 158 rad/s and ζ = 0.08) moves from q = 0 to qd = 0.5 rad. I simulated 0.3 s with error control and compared q with the exact solution every 0.03 s. "Relinearized" recomputes the linearization for each half-step, using h/2 and that half-step's starting state:

| Target accuracy | Steps, current | Max error, current | Steps, relinearized | Max error, relinearized |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1e-1 | 12 | 0.231 rad | 14 | 0.247 rad |
| 1e-2 | 24 | 0.231 rad | 35 | 0.161 rad |
| 1e-3 | 116 | 0.101 rad | 150 | 0.065 rad |
| 1e-4 | 529 | 0.040 rad | 589 | 0.025 rad |

The effect is real but modest. Relinearizing lets error control see more of the error, so it takes 11–46% more steps, and at the three tighter accuracies the error drops by about a third. Both versions are still far from the exact solution for this servo, so most of that error has some other source.

### Possible fix

Recompute the desired-state PD terms for each half-step, since they're analytic and cheap, and keep reusing the finite-difference linearizations of any other external systems. (The experiment simply relinearized everything, which costs nothing extra there because the plant has no other external systems.)

Is the current behavior an intended approximation? This came up in the review of #25290.

<details><summary>Experiment code</summary>

Added to `multibody/cenic/test/cenic_integrator_test.cc` on the #25290 branch, which provides `MakeServoedPin()`:

```cpp
GTEST_TEST(CenicIntegrator, HalfStepExperiment) {
  const double I = 0.004, Kp = 100.0, Kd = 0.1, qd = 0.5;
  const double wn = std::sqrt(Kp / I), zeta = Kd / (2 * std::sqrt(Kp * I));
  const double wd = wn * std::sqrt(1 - zeta * zeta);
  auto exact_q = [&](double t) {
    const double e0 = -qd;
    return qd + std::exp(-zeta * wn * t) *
                    (e0 * std::cos(wd * t) +
                     zeta * wn * e0 / wd * std::sin(wd * t));
  };
  std::cout << fmt::format("wn={:.1f} rad/s zeta={:.3f}\n", wn, zeta);
  for (const double accuracy : {1e-1, 1e-2, 1e-3, 1e-4}) {
    const std::unique_ptr<MultibodyPlant<double>> plant =
        MakeServoedPin(Kp, Kd);
    const auto& pin = plant->GetJointByName<RevoluteJoint>("pin");
    Simulator<double> simulator(*plant);
    Context<double>& context = simulator.get_mutable_context();
    plant->get_desired_state_input_port(default_model_instance())
        .FixValue(&context, Vector2d(qd, 0.0));
    auto& integrator = simulator.reset_integrator<CenicIntegrator<double>>();
    integrator.set_maximum_step_size(0.1);
    integrator.set_target_accuracy(accuracy);
    double max_err = 0;
    for (int k = 1; k <= 10; ++k) {
      const double t = 0.03 * k;
      simulator.AdvanceTo(t);
      max_err = std::max(max_err, std::abs(pin.get_angle(context) - exact_q(t)));
    }
    std::cout << fmt::format(
        "accuracy {:.0e}: steps {:5d}, max |q - q_exact| over 0.3 s = {:.2e} "
        "rad\n",
        accuracy, integrator.get_num_steps_taken(), max_err);
  }
}
```

The "relinearized" runs also applied this patch to `cenic_integrator.cc`:

